.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt CREATEHOMEDIR 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm createhomedir
.Nd create and populate home directories from the user template
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl a
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl u Ar user Op Fl u Ar user ...
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl i
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a user's home directory and fills it from the user template in
.Pa /System/Library/User Template .
.Pp
Home directories are normally created when the account is, by
.Xr adduser 8
or
.Xr pw 8 ,
and that covers network accounts too, because a network user is created on
the directory server, where the home lives and is exported to clients.
.Nm
therefore exists for repair: a home missing because a plist was restored,
hand-edited, or copied between machines.
.Xr login 1
degrades rather than failing in that case, reporting
.Dq No home directory. Logging in with home = / ,
so an affected user is not locked out and
.Nm
can be run afterwards.
.Pp
Users are enumerated with
.Xr getpwent 3 ,
so accounts are read through
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5
and any name source is treated alike.
Only homes under
.Pa /Local/Users
or
.Pa /Network/Users
are considered; system accounts in
.Xr master.passwd 5
point at
.Pa /var/empty ,
.Pa /nonexistent
and similar paths, which must stay absent.
.Pp
An existing home is left alone, and within a home an existing file is never
overwritten, so a user's own dot files outrank the template's.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Create homes for every user the system can enumerate.
This is the default when none of
.Fl a , u
or
.Fl i
is given.
.It Fl b
Consider both local and network homes.
This is the default.
.It Fl c
Consider only homes under
.Pa /Local/Users .
.It Fl i
Read user names from standard input, one per line.
.It Fl n
Dry run: report what would be done and change nothing.
This differs from Darwin, where
.Fl n
names a directory domain; NextBSD has no equivalent to select.
.It Fl s
Consider only homes under
.Pa /Network/Users .
.It Fl u Ar user
Create the home for
.Ar user .
May be repeated.
.It Fl v
Report each path as it is created.
.El
.Sh THE USER TEMPLATE
.Pa /System/Library/User Template/Non_localized
is copied into each new home.
Its directories each contain an empty
.Pa .localized
marker, which is both what lets the set be stored and shipped and Darwin's hook
for
displaying a translated folder name: the names on disk are the same in
every language, and only a file manager's display is localised.
.Pp
.Pa __permissions.plist
is applied afterwards, for the modes the copied tree cannot carry.
It is an array of
.Ql { Command , Argument , Paths }
applied in order, where
.Ql Command
is
.Cm CHMOD
or
.Cm CHGRP ,
and each path is relative to the home directory.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/System/Library/User Template/__permissions.plist" -compact
.It Pa /System/Library/User Template/Non_localized
the tree copied into each home
.It Pa /System/Library/User Template/__permissions.plist
modes applied after the copy
.It Pa /usr/share/skel
empty, as on
Darwin ;
the template replaces it
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Repair every missing home:
.Pp
.Dl # createhomedir -a -v
.Pp
See what would be created for one user, without changing anything:
.Pp
.Dl $ createhomedir -n -v -u jane
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command first appeared in
Mac OS X, in section 1 and driven by Open Directory domains.
The
.Nm
described here was written for NextBSD and reads accounts through the
name-service switch instead.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
does not create the account itself, and will not invent a home for a user the
system cannot resolve.
